  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "D8reNwD1BkrIHa1OXNLWhLYfvPQ=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:33:16.909Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Seidor's tool field records SAP, with the note that it is the largest Spanish SAP house, and Microsoft alongside. Its verticals are manufacturing, retail and banking.\n\nScale within one vendor's ecosystem is a different kind of occupancy from holding several partner badges. When a firm's market identity is its SAP practice, the platform choice is not a per-project decision but the thing clients hire it for, so the slot the pilot screens for is not merely filled \u2014 it is the product being sold.\n\nThe tier itself is mechanical: a provisional v2 tail entry rescored on size and slot occupancy, never scored on v3, with the flag still asking for an OSS-portfolio screen before anything is acted on. In Seidor's case the two inputs point the same way, which makes the exclusion better motivated than on the rows where the stack was never researched.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- Seidor (ES)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> DERIVED, NOT SCORED ON v3 \u2014 v2 row rescored mechanically (size + slot-occupancy only). Run the OSS-portfolio screen before acting. v2 verticals: Manufacturing, retail, banking",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:33:16.909973Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:33:16.909978Z"
    }
  },
